# How do we measure how rich a country is?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What does GDP count, and why per person? Why compare countries at local prices (PPP) rather than exchange rates?"
next: {"title":"Is GDP the right measure of how well we live?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/11-is-gdp-the-right-measure-of-how-well-we.cast"}

## A year's output
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - United States
      - China
      - India
      - Norway
    values:
      - 27.8
      - 18.3
      - 3.5
      - 0.5
    value_labels: true
    bar_colors: same
    y_label: GDP, $ trillion (2023)
sources:
    - id: wdi_gdp
      title: 'World Development Indicators: GDP (current US$), NY.GDP.MKTP.CD'
      authors: World Bank
      year: 2025
      finding: 'GDP in 2023 at market exchange rates: United States $27.8 trillion, China $18.3 trillion, India $3.5 trillion, Norway $0.50 trillion'
      url: https://data.worldbank.org/indicator/NY.GDP.MKTP.CD
    - id: wdi_pop
      title: 'World Development Indicators: Population, total (SP.POP.TOTL)'
      authors: World Bank
      year: 2025
      finding: 'Population 2023: India 1.44 billion, Norway 5.5 million'
      url: https://data.worldbank.org/indicator/SP.POP.TOTL

    scratch loaf top-right work ["flour $40 → bread $100","sales: 40 + 100 = 140 ✗","value added: 40 + 60 = 100","= wages + profits"] font_size 30 hidden true
    scratch def top-right work ["GDP:","final goods & services","one year · market prices"] font_size 30 hidden true
    scratch pop top-right cites ["wdi_pop"] work ["India: 7 × Norway's GDP","India: 1.44 billion people","Norway: 5.5 million"] font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "A year's output"

How do we measure how rich a country is? Most people reach for one number: GDP.
    draw axes

Here it is for four count (@draw bar_1@)ries in 2023, in US dollars. The United States: about twenty-eight trillion.

Chi (@draw bar_2@)na, about eighteen tri (@draw bar_3@)llion. India, three and a half.

An (@draw bar_4@)d Norway: half a trillion, barely a sliver.

    pause 0.3

B: Fine, but a total of what, exactly? Money in the bank?
    highlight axes effect underline

Not wealth: a year's production. Take one loaf of (@draw loaf_box@) bread in Harbourtown, with illustrative prices.

The town's miller sells Mia forty dollars of flour, and she sells the loaf for a hundred.
    draw loaf_line_1

    quiz "How much does this add to Harbourtown's GDP?" store q1
        right: A hundred: only the final loaf counts.
        wrong: The flour is already inside the loaf.
        * $140
        + $100
        * $40

B: I went for a hundred and forty, both sales. But that counts the flour twice, doesn't it? It's already inside the loaf.
    draw loaf_line_2

Exactly. GDP counts only the final good: the hundred-dollar loaf.
    highlight loaf_line_1 part "bread $100" effect circle

That hundred is forty dollars added by the miller plus sixty added by Mia: the value each of them added.
    draw loaf_line_3

Add up what they earned instead, wages an (@draw loaf_line_4@)d profits, and you get the same hundred. Output and income are one number seen from two sides.

    erase loaf

So GDP is the market value of all the final goods and services a country produces in a year.
    draw def_box def_line_1 def_line_2 def_line_3

    erase def

B: Then India's economy is seven times Norway's. Is the average Indian seven times richer?
    highlight bar_3 bar_4

Far from it. India's total is shared by one point four billion people; Norway's by five and a half million.
    draw pop_box pop_line_1 pop_line_2 pop_line_3

    pause 0.8

## Per person, at local prices
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - United States
      - China
      - India
      - Norway
    values:
      - [82.6, 13, 2.4, 91]
      - [82.6, 25.2, 9.9, 107.8]
    stage: 0
    value_labels: true
    bar_colors: same
    y_label: GDP per person, $ thousand (2023)
sources:
    - id: wdi_pc
      title: 'World Development Indicators: GDP per capita (current US$), NY.GDP.PCAP.CD'
      authors: World Bank
      year: 2025
      finding: '2023, market exchange rates: United States $82,600, China $13,000, India $2,430, Norway $91,000'
      url: https://data.worldbank.org/indicator/NY.GDP.PCAP.CD
    - id: wdi_ppp
      title: 'World Development Indicators: GDP per capita, PPP (current international $), NY.GDP.PCAP.PP.CD'
      authors: World Bank / International Comparison Program
      year: 2025
      finding: '2023, at purchasing power parity: United States $82,600, China $25,200, India $9,850, Norway $107,800'
      url: https://data.worldbank.org/indicator/NY.GDP.PCAP.PP.CD

    text rate_mkt "at market exchange rates" at.percent [55,88] cites ["wdi_pc"] hidden true
    text rate_ppp "at PPP (international $)" at.percent [55,88] cites ["wdi_ppp"] hidden true
    scratch hair top-right work ["a haircut:","Delhi ≪ New York"] font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "Per person, at local prices"

B: So divide each total by its people.
    draw axes rate_mkt

B: The Un (@draw bar_1@)ited States: about eighty-three thousand dollars a head.

B: China: about thirteen thousand.
    draw bar_2

    ask "Drag India's bar to your guess." on bar_3 store g default "20" voice b
        intro: Before India's bar rises, drag it to your guess.
        right: Close: India's GDP per person was about $2,400.
        wrong: Lower still: about $2,400.

B: About twenty-four hundred dollars: less than a thirtieth of the American figure.
    highlight bar_3

B: And Norway, a sliver a m (@draw bar_4@)oment ago, jumps to the top: about ninety-one thousand.

    pause 0.3

Twenty-four hundred dollars a year. Could anyone live on that in New York?
    highlight bar_3 effect circle

    erase rate_mkt

B: Not in New York. But in India a haircut, a bus ride or a plate of food costs a fraction of what it costs there.
    draw hair_box hair_line_1 hair_line_2

So the exchange rate makes poor countries look poorer than they live?
    highlight bar_3 bar_2

    erase hair

B: Yes. So economists convert at purchasing power parity, (@draw rate_ppp@) PPP: the rate at which the same basket of goods costs the same in both countries.

B: The answer comes in international dollars, which buy in each country what one dollar buys in America.
    animate stage 1 duration 4 ghost true

India's bar quadruples, to almost ten thousand. China's doubles, to about twenty-five thousand.
    highlight bar_3 bar_2

B: And the American bar stays put: in America, an international dollar is simply a dollar.
    highlight bar_1 effect box

Pause and slide between the two yourself. Watch which bars move most.
    explore params ["stage"]

B: The poorest, because haircuts and rents can't be shipped abroad. Where wages are low, they stay cheap.
    highlight bar_3 effect pulse

    pause 0.8

## How far to trust it
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - China
      - India
    values:
      - [8.8, 3.8]
      - [5.3, 2.3]
    stage: 0
    value_labels: true
    bar_colors: same
    gap: 0.6
    y_label: GDP 2005 at PPP, int'l $ trillion
sources:
    - id: crs2008
      title: How Large is China's Economy? Does it Matter? (CRS Report RS22808)
      authors: Morrison, W. M. & Martin, M. F.
      year: 2008
      finding: The World Bank's December 2007 ICP results cut its estimate of China's 2005 GDP at PPP by 40%, from $8.8 trillion to $5.3 trillion, reflecting China's first participation in the price survey; India's estimate fell by about 40% too
      url: https://www.everycrsreport.com/reports/RS22808.html
    - id: merco2007
      title: W. Bank downsized India's economy to fifth world largest
      authors: MercoPress
      year: 2007
      finding: After the revision India's 2005 GDP at PPP was $2.34 trillion, against $3.8 trillion before
      url: https://en.mercopress.com/2007/12/20/w-bank-downsized-india-s-economy-to-fifth-world-largest
    - id: wdi_conv
      title: 'World Development Indicators: PPP conversion factor (PA.NUS.PPP), official exchange rate (PA.NUS.FCRF), GDP per capita (NY.GDP.PCAP.CD)'
      authors: World Bank
      year: 2025
      finding: 'India 2023: GDP per person about ₹201,000; 82.6 rupees per US dollar at the market rate; 20.5 rupees per international dollar at PPP'
      url: https://data.worldbank.org/indicator/PA.NUS.PPP

    scratch use top-right work ["buying abroad → market rate","how people live → PPP"] font_size 28 hidden true
    scratch sure top-right work ["PPP for living standards: consensus","exact levels: mostly agreed"] font_size 28 hidden true
    scratch math top-right cites ["wdi_conv"] work ["India 2023: ₹201,000 per person","÷ 82.6 ₹ per $ ≈ $2,430","÷ 20.5 ₹ per int'l $ ≈ $9,800"] font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "How far to trust it"

B: Here's my worry. Who decides what a haircut costs in Delhi?
    draw axes

Price surveys: thousands of prices, collected in most of the world's countries every few years. And they can move the answer a lot.
    highlight axes effect underline

Take China in 2005. Before its first f (@draw bar_1@)ull price survey, its economy was put at eight point eight trillion international do (@draw bar_2 ends@)llars.

Then, in December 2007, the survey results came in, and the estimate fell by forty percent, to five point three trillion.
    animate stage 1 duration 3 ghost true

B: Same China, same year, same factories. Only the measured prices changed.
    highlight bar_1 effect box

India's estimate fell by almost as much: from three point eight to two point three trillion.
    highlight bar_2 effect box

B: And another thing. When India bu (@draw use_box use_line_1@)ys oil or aircraft abroad, it pays at the market exchange rate, not at PPP.

True. Market rates for what a country can buy (@draw use_line_2@) from the world; PPP for how its people live.

    erase use

How sure are we? That PPP is the (@draw sure_box sure_line_1@) right yardstick for living standards: consensus.

The exact levels: mostly agreed, with real margins of error.
    draw sure_line_2

    erase sure

    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test

    ask "Show the conversion worked out, or skip it?" on mathq stage own voice b
        intro: Want to see a conversion worked out?

@math

    erase mathq

Income per person in India in 2023 was about two hundred and one thousand rupees. Divide by about eighty-three rupees per dollar, the market rate, and you get about twenty-four hundred dollars.
    draw math_box math_line_1 math_line_2

Divide by twenty and a half rupees, the PPP rate, and you get about ninety-eight hundred: the same rupees, four times the dollars.
    draw math_line_3

    erase math

@test

    erase mathq

    quiz "A carpenter buys wood for $300 and sells the table for $800. How much does the carpenter's work add to GDP?" intro "Three new cases."
        right: $500: the value the carpenter added to the wood.
        wrong: What did the carpenter add to the wood?
        * $300
        + $500
        * $800
        * $1,100

    quiz "Country X's income per person is $4,000 at market exchange rates, and its prices are about half America's. Roughly what is it at PPP?" voice b
        right: About $8,000: at half the prices, each dollar buys twice as much.
        wrong: If things cost half as much, how much does each dollar buy?
        * $2,000
        * $4,000
        + $8,000

    quiz "A government wants to know how many foreign-made vaccines its economy could pay for. Which measure fits?"
        right: Market rates: vaccines bought abroad are paid for at world prices.
        wrong: Who sets the price of a vaccine bought abroad?
        + GDP at market exchange rates
        * GDP at PPP

So a country's riches are measured as a year's output per person, at the prices its people actually pay, and measured with error. Whether that number tells us how well people live is a question of its own.
    highlight bar_1 bar_2

    pause 0.8

